var e={id:`nsulphur`,objectives:[`Explain the lack of reactivity of nitrogen in terms of the N≡N triple bond energy`,`Describe the formation and structure of the ammonium ion (dative bond) and its reaction with alkalis`,`Explain the Haber process conditions (450°C, 200 atm, Fe) using principles of kinetics and equilibria`,`Explain the Contact process conditions (450°C, 1–2 atm, V₂O₅) using principles of kinetics and equilibria`,`Recognise the industrial importance of NH₃ (fertilisers, nitric acid) and H₂SO₄ (fertilisers, detergents, batteries)`,`Describe environmental consequences of NOₓ (acid rain, smog, eutrophication) and SO₂ (acid rain, respiratory problems)`,`Explain the catalytic removal of NOₓ in car exhausts and methods of controlling SO₂ emissions`,`Describe the use of SO₂ in food preservation`],chapters:[{id:`ns1`,title:`Why Is Nitrogen So Unreactive?`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Nitrogen gas (N₂) makes up 78% of the atmosphere yet is remarkably unreactive at room temperature. This is because the two nitrogen atoms are joined by a TRIPLE BOND (N≡N) with a bond energy of 944 kJ mol⁻¹ — one of the strongest covalent bonds known. The very high activation energy required to break this bond makes N₂ kinetically inert.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`WHY IS N₂ UNREACTIVE?`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:70,primitives:[{p:`circle`,cx:120,cy:35,r:20,fill:`primary`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1.5,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:120,y:39,text:`N`,size:14,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:142,y1:27,x2:178,y2:27,color:`text`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:142,y1:35,x2:178,y2:35,color:`text`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:142,y1:43,x2:178,y2:43,color:`text`,width:2},{p:`circle`,cx:200,cy:35,r:20,fill:`primary`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1.5,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:200,y:39,text:`N`,size:14,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:15,text:`TRIPLE BOND`,size:9,bold:!0,color:`accent`}]},{type:`equation`,text:`Bond energy: 944 kJ mol⁻¹`,big:!0},{type:`note`,text:`one of the strongest bonds in chemistry`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`N₂ is kinetically inert — very high Ea to break N≡N`}]},caption:`The N≡N triple bond is exceptionally strong`},{type:`h`,text:`Chemical properties of nitrogen`},{type:`ul`,items:[`N₂ reacts with H₂ at high temperature and pressure, with a catalyst: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (Haber process).`,`N₂ reacts with O₂ only at very high temperatures (>2000 °C), such as in lightning or car engines: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO.`,`N₂ reacts with some reactive metals (Li, Mg) when heated: 3Mg + N₂ → Mg₃N₂ (magnesium nitride).`,`Overall, the high bond dissociation energy of N≡N means most reactions involving N₂ require extreme conditions.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Comparison with oxygen`},{type:`p`,text:`O₂ has a double bond (O=O, 496 kJ mol⁻¹) which is much weaker than N≡N. This is why oxygen is far more reactive than nitrogen at room temperature — it supports combustion, rusting and respiration, while nitrogen is essentially inert under the same conditions.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`N₂ has a triple bond (944 kJ mol⁻¹) — very strong, very high Ea.`,`N₂ reacts only at high T/P or with very reactive metals.`,`Kinetically inert despite being thermodynamically reactive in some cases.`]}]},{id:`ns2`,title:`Ammonia & the Ammonium Ion`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Ammonia (NH₃) is a colourless gas with a pungent smell. It is the primary nitrogen compound of industrial importance. NH₃ has a lone pair on nitrogen which makes it a BASE (proton acceptor) and allows it to form the ammonium ion (NH₄⁺) by dative bonding.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`THE AMMONIUM ION · NH₄⁺`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:130,primitives:[{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:65,r:16,fill:`primary`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1.5,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:69,text:`N`,size:12,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:49,x2:160,y2:25,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:25,r:10,fill:`accent`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1,color:`accent`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:29,text:`H`,size:10,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:176,y1:65,x2:200,y2:65,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`circle`,cx:200,cy:65,r:10,fill:`accent`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1,color:`accent`},{p:`text`,x:200,y:69,text:`H`,size:10,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:81,x2:160,y2:105,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:105,r:10,fill:`accent`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1,color:`accent`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:109,text:`H`,size:10,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:144,y1:65,x2:120,y2:65,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`circle`,cx:120,cy:65,r:10,fill:`accent`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1,color:`accent`},{p:`text`,x:120,y:69,text:`H`,size:10,bold:!0,color:`text`},{p:`text`,x:215,y:30,text:`+`,size:14,bold:!0,color:`pink`},{p:`text`,x:235,y:65,text:`← dative bond`,size:8,anchor:`start`,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:235,y:78,text:`(N lone pair → H⁺)`,size:8,anchor:`start`,color:`primary`}]},{type:`note`,text:`NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺ (dative/coordinate bond)`,emphasis:!0},{type:`note`,text:`tetrahedral shape · bond angle 109.5°`},{type:`note`,text:`all four N–H bonds identical once formed`}]},caption:`Formation and structure of NH₄⁺`},{type:`h`,text:`Formation of the ammonium ion`},{type:`ul`,items:[`NH₃ has a lone pair on N. When NH₃ acts as a base, it donates this lone pair to an H⁺ ion: NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺.`,`The bond formed is a DATIVE (coordinate) bond — both electrons come from the N lone pair.`,`Once formed, all four N–H bonds in NH₄⁺ are identical and indistinguishable.`,`NH₄⁺ is tetrahedral with bond angle 109.5°.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reactions of NH₄⁺ with alkalis`},{type:`ul`,items:[`NH₄⁺ salts react with alkalis on heating to release ammonia gas:`,`NH₄Cl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O + NH₃↑ (test: damp red litmus turns blue).`,`This is the standard test for ammonium ions.`,`General equation: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Laboratory preparation of ammonia`},{type:`p`,text:`Heat any ammonium salt with a strong base (e.g. Ca(OH)₂): 2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2H₂O + 2NH₃↑. Collect by upward delivery (NH₃ is less dense than air). Dry over CaO (not CaCl₂, which reacts with NH₃).`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺ via dative bond from N lone pair.`,`NH₄⁺ is tetrahedral (109.5°).`,`Test: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ (turns damp red litmus blue).`]}]},{id:`ns3`,title:`The Haber Process`,minutes:12,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The Haber process manufactures ammonia from nitrogen and hydrogen: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹. It is one of the most important industrial processes in the world — ammonia is used to make fertilisers, nitric acid, explosives and nylon.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`THE HABER PROCESS`,blocks:[{type:`equation`,text:`N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)   ΔH = −92 kJ`,big:!0},{type:`panel`,title:`INDUSTRIAL CONDITIONS`,body:[`Temperature: 450 °C`,`Pressure: 200 atm · Catalyst: iron (Fe)`],color:`primary`},{type:`note`,text:`WHY THESE CONDITIONS?`,emphasis:!0},{type:`compare`,left:{title:`TEMPERATURE`,body:[`low T → high yield (exo)`,`BUT too slow; 450°C is`,`a COMPROMISE`],color:`pink`},right:{title:`PRESSURE`,body:[`4 mol gas → 2 mol gas`,`high P shifts right`,`200 atm: cost vs yield`],color:`cyan`}},{type:`note`,text:`Fe catalyst: increases RATE, does NOT change K or yield`},{type:`note`,text:`unreacted N₂ and H₂ are RECYCLED back through the reactor`},{type:`note`,text:`NH₃ is cooled and liquefied — shifts equilibrium right`},{type:`note`,text:`yield per pass ≈ 15%, but overall yield ≈ 98% with recycling`}]},caption:`Haber process: conditions and rationale`},{type:`h`,text:`Conditions explained using equilibria`},{type:`ul`,items:[`TEMPERATURE: the forward reaction is EXOTHERMIC. Le Chatelier says low temperature favours products (more NH₃). But at low temperature, the rate is too slow (particles lack energy to overcome the very high Ea of breaking N≡N). The compromise is 450 °C — fast enough for industry, reasonable yield.`,`PRESSURE: 4 moles of gas on the left, 2 on the right. High pressure shifts equilibrium RIGHT (fewer moles). 200 atm is used — higher would give more yield but is too expensive and dangerous.`,`CATALYST: iron (Fe) catalyst lowers the Ea so equilibrium is reached faster. It does NOT change the position of equilibrium or the value of K.`,`REMOVAL: NH₃ is continuously cooled and condensed out as a liquid. Removing product shifts equilibrium RIGHT. Unreacted N₂ and H₂ are RECYCLED.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Conditions explained using kinetics`},{type:`ul`,items:[`The N≡N bond (944 kJ mol⁻¹) has a very high Ea to break. Without the catalyst, the reaction would be impossibly slow.`,`The iron catalyst provides a surface for N₂ and H₂ to ADSORB. The N≡N bond is weakened on the surface (heterogeneous catalysis), allowing reaction at a feasible temperature.`,`At 450 °C, enough molecules have energy ≥ Ea (Boltzmann distribution) for a workable rate.`,`Yield per pass is only about 15%, but recycling gives an overall conversion of about 98%.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Exam template`,body:`For any "explain the conditions" question, address THREE factors: (1) Temperature — compromise between yield and rate. (2) Pressure — Le Chatelier and cost/safety. (3) Catalyst — increases rate via lower Ea, does NOT change K or yield.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`450 °C (compromise: rate vs yield), 200 atm (fewer gas moles), Fe catalyst (speeds up).`,`NH₃ condensed and removed; unreacted gases recycled.`,`Yield per pass ~15%; overall ~98% with recycling.`]}]},{id:`ns4`,title:`The Contact Process`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The Contact Process manufactures sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) — the most widely used industrial chemical. It has three stages, with the second (catalytic oxidation of SO₂) being the key equilibrium step.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`THE CONTACT PROCESS`,subtitle:`manufacture of sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄)`,blocks:[{type:`panel`,title:`STAGE 1 — BURN SULPHUR`,body:[`S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g)`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`STAGE 2 — CATALYTIC OXIDATION (KEY STEP)`,body:[`2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)   ΔH = −196 kJ`,`450 °C · 1–2 atm · V₂O₅ catalyst`],color:`primary`},{type:`panel`,title:`STAGE 3 — ABSORB SO₃ IN CONC. H₂SO₄`,body:[`SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇ (oleum)`,`H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄`],color:`accent`},{type:`panel`,title:`WHY DISSOLVE IN H₂SO₄, NOT WATER?`,body:[`SO₃ + H₂O directly → dangerous acid mist`,`dissolving in conc. H₂SO₄ is controlled and safe`],color:`pink`},{type:`note`,text:`Low P fine: equilibrium already favours products (Kp large)`},{type:`note`,text:`450°C compromise: rate vs yield (same logic as Haber)`}]},caption:`Three stages of the Contact Process`},{type:`h`,text:`Stage 1: Burn sulphur`},{type:`p`,text:`S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g). Sulphur is burned in excess air. This is exothermic and goes to completion (not reversible). The SO₂ is purified to prevent catalyst poisoning.`},{type:`h`,text:`Stage 2: Catalytic oxidation (key step)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), ΔH = −196 kJ mol⁻¹.`,`Temperature: 450 °C — compromise (exothermic forward, so low T gives high yield, but rate too slow at low T).`,`Pressure: 1–2 atm is sufficient because Kp is already large (equilibrium strongly favours SO₃). Higher pressure is unnecessary and expensive.`,`Catalyst: vanadium(V) oxide (V₂O₅). Heterogeneous catalyst — SO₂ and O₂ adsorb on its surface.`,`Conversion is about 96–98% in modern multi-bed converters.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Stage 3: Absorption`},{type:`ul`,items:[`SO₃ is dissolved in CONCENTRATED H₂SO₄ (not water!) to form oleum (H₂S₂O₇): SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇.`,`Oleum is then carefully diluted with water: H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄.`,`Why not dissolve SO₃ directly in water? Because SO₃ + H₂O is extremely exothermic and produces a dangerous acid MIST that is difficult to contain. Dissolving in conc. H₂SO₄ is controllable and safe.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Stage 1: S + O₂ → SO₂. Stage 2: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ (V₂O₅, 450°C, 1–2 atm). Stage 3: SO₃ → oleum → H₂SO₄.`,`Low pressure works because Kp is large.`,`SO₃ dissolved in H₂SO₄, not water (avoids acid mist).`]}]},{id:`ns5`,title:`Industrial Importance of NH₃ & H₂SO₄`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Ammonia and sulphuric acid are among the most produced chemicals globally. Their uses span agriculture, manufacturing and defence.`},{type:`h`,text:`Uses of ammonia`},{type:`ul`,items:[`FERTILISERS (by far the largest use): ammonium nitrate (NH₄NO₃), ammonium sulphate ((NH₄)₂SO₄), urea ((NH₂)₂CO). These provide nitrogen for plant growth.`,`Nitric acid manufacture (Ostwald process): 4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O (Pt catalyst), followed by oxidation of NO to NO₂ and dissolution in water.`,`Nylon production: ammonia is a starting material for hexanediamine.`,`Cleaning products: ammonia solution is a household cleaner and degreaser.`,`Explosives: ammonium nitrate is used as an explosive in mining.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Uses of sulphuric acid`},{type:`ul`,items:[`FERTILISERS: ammonium sulphate, superphosphate (treating rock phosphate with H₂SO₄).`,`Detergents and soaps.`,`Paints and pigments (TiO₂ production).`,`Lead-acid car batteries (electrolyte is dilute H₂SO₄).`,`Manufacturing other chemicals: HCl, HF, phosphoric acid.`,`Steel pickling: removing rust/scale from steel surfaces.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`NH₃: fertilisers (main), nitric acid, nylon, explosives, cleaning.`,`H₂SO₄: fertilisers, detergents, paints, batteries, chemical manufacture.`]}]},{id:`ns6`,title:`Environmental Impacts of N & S Compounds`,minutes:10,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Compounds of nitrogen and sulphur released into the atmosphere cause acid rain, smog, and damage to health and ecosystems. Understanding these impacts is a significant part of the syllabus.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACTS`,blocks:[{type:`compare`,left:{title:`NITROGEN`,body:[`NOₓ → acid rain (HNO₃)`,`NO₂ → photochemical smog`,`NO₂ → respiratory irritant`,`nitrates → eutrophication`,`(algal blooms, O₂ depletion)`],color:`pink`},right:{title:`SULPHUR`,body:[`SO₂ → acid rain (H₂SO₃)`,`SO₃ → acid rain (H₂SO₄)`,`SO₂ → respiratory problems`,`corrodes buildings, statues`,`damages ecosystems (lakes)`],color:`amber`}},{type:`panel`,title:`SOLUTIONS`,body:[`catalytic converters: 2NO → N₂ + O₂ (Pt/Rh)`,`flue gas desulphurisation: SO₂ + CaO → CaSO₃`,`alkaline scrubbing: SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O`,`limestone fluidised beds: CaCO₃ absorbs SO₂`],color:`green`},{type:`note`,text:`lean-burn engines reduce NOₓ by using excess air`}]},caption:`Environmental impacts and solutions`},{type:`h`,text:`Nitrogen oxides (NOₓ)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Sources: car engines (N₂ + O₂ → 2NO at high T), power stations, lightning.`,`NO is colourless; it reacts with O₂ to form NO₂ (brown, toxic): 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂.`,`NO₂ dissolves in water to form nitric acid: 4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃ (acid rain).`,`NO₂ contributes to PHOTOCHEMICAL SMOG — a brown haze over cities, causing respiratory problems.`,`Nitrates from fertilisers leach into waterways causing EUTROPHICATION: algal bloom → algae die → bacteria decompose algae using O₂ → aquatic life suffocates.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Sulphur dioxide (SO₂)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Sources: burning fossil fuels (coal and oil contain sulphur impurities), volcanic emissions, smelting sulphide ores.`,`SO₂ dissolves in water: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (sulphurous acid).`,`In the atmosphere, SO₂ is oxidised to SO₃ (catalysed by NOₓ): SO₂ + ½O₂ → SO₃, then SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄.`,`H₂SO₃ and H₂SO₄ in rain → ACID RAIN: corrodes limestone buildings, damages forests, acidifies lakes (kills fish).`,`SO₂ is a respiratory irritant — causes breathing difficulties, worsens asthma.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`NOₓ: acid rain (HNO₃), photochemical smog, eutrophication from nitrate runoff.`,`SO₂/SO₃: acid rain (H₂SO₃/H₂SO₄), corrosion, respiratory irritant.`]}]},{id:`ns7`,title:`Catalytic Removal of NOₓ`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Catalytic converters in car exhausts are the primary method for removing NOₓ emissions. They use precious metal catalysts (Pt, Pd, Rh) to convert toxic gases into harmless ones.`},{type:`d`,spec:{title:`CATALYTIC CONVERTER`,blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:80,primitives:[{p:`rect`,x:20,y:25,w:60,h:30,rx:4,fill:`dim`,fillAlpha:.2,stroke:1,color:`dim`},{p:`text`,x:50,y:44,text:`exhaust`,size:7,color:`text`},{p:`rect`,x:100,y:15,w:120,h:50,rx:8,fill:`primary`,fillAlpha:.15,stroke:1.5,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:33,text:`Pt / Pd / Rh`,size:8,bold:!0,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:47,text:`honeycomb structure`,size:7,color:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:59,text:`large surface area`,size:6,color:`dim`},{p:`rect`,x:240,y:25,w:60,h:30,rx:4,fill:`green`,fillAlpha:.15,stroke:1,color:`green`},{p:`text`,x:270,y:44,text:`clean`,size:7,color:`green`},{p:`line`,x1:82,y1:40,x2:96,y2:40,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`path`,d:`M 96 40 L 92 37 L 92 43 Z`,fill:`text`,color:`text`},{p:`line`,x1:222,y1:40,x2:236,y2:40,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`path`,d:`M 236 40 L 232 37 L 232 43 Z`,fill:`text`,color:`text`}]},{type:`panel`,title:`KEY REACTIONS`,body:[`2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂`,`2NO → N₂ + O₂ (reduction of NOₓ)`,`unburnt HC + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O`],color:`primary`},{type:`note`,text:`heterogeneous catalysis: Pt/Rh surface`}]},caption:`Catalytic converter: heterogeneous catalysis`},{type:`h`,text:`Key reactions in the catalytic converter`},{type:`ul`,items:[`2CO(g) + 2NO(g) → 2CO₂(g) + N₂(g). CO and NO are both removed — CO is oxidised, NO is reduced.`,`Unburnt hydrocarbons + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Complete combustion of unburned fuel.`,`The catalyst (Pt/Pd/Rh) provides a large surface area (honeycomb structure) for adsorption of gases.`,`This is HETEROGENEOUS catalysis: solid catalyst, gaseous reactants.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Other NOₓ reduction methods`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Lean-burn engines: use a high air-to-fuel ratio. The lower combustion temperature produces less NOₓ (N₂ + O₂ reaction is less favoured at lower T).`,`Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR): recirculates some exhaust back into the engine, reducing peak flame temperature.`,`Selective catalytic reduction (SCR): injects urea solution into the exhaust, which reacts with NOₓ on a catalyst to produce N₂ and H₂O.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Catalyst poisoning`,body:`Lead in petrol POISONS the Pt/Rh catalyst by adsorbing onto active sites and blocking them. This is why leaded petrol has been banned in most countries — it would destroy the catalytic converter.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Catalytic converter: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂ (Pt/Pd/Rh surface).`,`Lean-burn engines: lower T → less NOₓ.`,`Lead poisons the catalyst → leaded petrol banned.`]}]},{id:`ns8`,title:`SO₂ in Food Preservation & Emission Control`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Sulphur dioxide has a dual role: it is an environmental pollutant but also a useful chemical in food preservation.`},{type:`h`,text:`SO₂ as a food preservative`},{type:`ul`,items:[`SO₂ (or sulphites like Na₂SO₃) is added to dried fruits, wine, fruit juices and some processed foods.`,`It acts as an ANTIOXIDANT — it reacts with oxygen before the food can, preventing browning and spoilage.`,`It also has ANTIMICROBIAL properties — it inhibits the growth of bacteria and moulds.`,`Labelled on food packaging as E220 (SO₂) or E221-E228 (various sulphites).`,`Some people are sensitive to sulphites — they can trigger asthma attacks.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Controlling SO₂ emissions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Flue gas desulphurisation (FGD): SO₂ in power station exhaust is removed by reacting with CaO or CaCO₃.`,`CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃. Or: CaCO₃ + SO₂ → CaSO₃ + CO₂.`,`Alkaline scrubbing: SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O.`,`Limestone-based fluidised beds: powdered CaCO₃ is mixed with the fuel so SO₂ is absorbed as it forms.`,`Using low-sulphur fuels or switching to natural gas (which contains almost no sulphur).`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`SO₂ preserves food: antioxidant + antimicrobial.`,`FGD: CaO/CaCO₃ + SO₂ → CaSO₃. Alkaline scrubbing: NaOH + SO₂ → Na₂SO₃.`,`Low-sulphur fuels reduce emissions at source.`]}]},{id:`ns9`,title:`Preparing Ammonium Sulphate`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Ammonium sulphate ((NH₄)₂SO₄) is an important nitrogenous fertiliser. It can be made in the lab by reacting ammonia solution with dilute sulphuric acid.`},{type:`h`,text:`Laboratory preparation`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: 2NH₃(aq) + H₂SO₄(aq) → (NH₄)₂SO₄(aq).`,`Method: titrate NH₃ solution with dilute H₂SO₄ using a suitable indicator (methyl orange) to find the endpoint.`,`Repeat WITHOUT indicator using the measured volumes to get a pure solution.`,`Evaporate carefully to crystallisation point, then cool to collect crystals.`,`Filter, wash with a little cold distilled water, and dry between filter papers.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why ammonium sulphate is a good fertiliser`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Contains nitrogen in a form (NH₄⁺) that plants can directly absorb through their roots.`,`Also contains sulphur (as SO₄²⁻), which is a secondary nutrient for plants.`,`Soluble in water → readily reaches plant roots.`,`Relatively stable — does not decompose or evaporate easily.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄ (acid-base neutralisation).`,`Lab method: titration → repeat without indicator → crystallise.`,`Important fertiliser: provides N (as NH₄⁺) and S (as SO₄²⁻).`]}]},{id:`ns10`,title:`Exam Technique & Common Pitfalls`,minutes:7,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`N & S questions combine industrial processes, equilibria/kinetics, and environmental chemistry. Here are the key patterns.`},{type:`h`,text:`Process conditions — the template`},{type:`ul`,items:[`For BOTH Haber and Contact: the answer template is: (1) Temperature is a COMPROMISE between rate and yield. (2) Pressure effect explained by Le Chatelier and moles of gas. (3) Catalyst speeds up attainment of equilibrium but does NOT change K or yield.`,`Haber: 450 °C, 200 atm, Fe. Contact: 450 °C, 1–2 atm, V₂O₅.`,`Key difference: Contact process uses LOW pressure because Kp is already favourable.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common mistakes`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Saying the catalyst "increases yield" — WRONG. It increases RATE only.`,`Confusing the Contact process catalyst (V₂O₅) with the Haber catalyst (Fe).`,`Forgetting that SO₃ is dissolved in H₂SO₄ (not water) in Stage 3 of Contact.`,`Not mentioning RECYCLING of unreacted gases in the Haber process.`,`Describing eutrophication without explaining the full chain: nitrate → algal bloom → decay → O₂ depletion → aquatic death.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Environmental equations to know`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Catalytic converter: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂.`,`FGD: CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃.`,`Alkaline scrubbing: SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O.`,`Acid rain formation: SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃; 4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Catalyst ≠ more yield. Catalyst = faster equilibrium.`,`Haber: Fe, 200 atm. Contact: V₂O₅, 1–2 atm.`,`SO₃ into H₂SO₄ (not water). Unreacted gases recycled.`]}]}],quizzes:[{q:`N₂ is unreactive because:`,opts:[`It has a single bond`,`It has a very strong triple bond (944 kJ mol⁻¹)`,`It is a noble gas`,`It has no electrons`],ans:1,hint:`The N≡N triple bond is one of the strongest known.`},{q:`The bond in NH₃ → NH₄⁺ is:`,opts:[`Ionic`,`Covalent`,`Dative (coordinate)`,`Metallic`],ans:2,hint:`N donates its lone pair to H⁺.`},{q:`The shape of NH₄⁺ is:`,opts:[`Pyramidal`,`Tetrahedral`,`Linear`,`Trigonal planar`],ans:1,hint:`4 bonding pairs, no lone pairs → tetrahedral.`},{q:`The test for NH₄⁺ ions involves:`,opts:[`Adding AgNO₃`,`Heating with NaOH → NH₃ turns damp red litmus blue`,`Adding BaCl₂`,`Burning the sample`],ans:1,hint:`NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O on heating.`},{q:`In the Haber process, the catalyst is:`,opts:[`V₂O₅`,`Pt`,`Fe`,`MnO₂`],ans:2,hint:`Iron is the heterogeneous catalyst for N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃.`},{q:`The temperature in the Haber process (450 °C) is described as a:`,opts:[`Maximum`,`Minimum`,`Compromise`,`Standard`],ans:2,hint:`Balances rate (needs high T) against yield (favoured by low T).`},{q:`High pressure in the Haber process favours NH₃ because:`,opts:[`There are more moles of gas on the right`,`There are fewer moles of gas on the right`,`Pressure has no effect`,`The catalyst works better`],ans:1,hint:`4 mol gas → 2 mol gas. Le Chatelier: high P favours fewer moles.`},{q:`The catalyst in the Haber process:`,opts:[`Increases yield`,`Increases rate only`,`Changes K`,`Shifts equilibrium right`],ans:1,hint:`Catalyst speeds up both forward and reverse equally.`},{q:`Unreacted gases in the Haber process are:`,opts:[`Discarded`,`Recycled`,`Burned`,`Stored`],ans:1,hint:`Recycling increases overall conversion to ~98%.`},{q:`In the Contact process, the catalyst is:`,opts:[`Fe`,`V₂O₅`,`Pt`,`Ni`],ans:1,hint:`Vanadium(V) oxide catalyses 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.`},{q:`Why is low pressure used in the Contact process?`,opts:[`To increase yield`,`Kp is already large so equilibrium strongly favours products`,`To save catalyst`,`Le Chatelier says low P is better`],ans:1,hint:`The equilibrium already lies far to the right; high P is unnecessary.`},{q:`SO₃ is dissolved in conc. H₂SO₄ rather than water because:`,opts:[`It is cheaper`,`Direct dissolution in water creates a dangerous acid mist`,`H₂SO₄ is a better solvent`,`Water is too cold`],ans:1,hint:`SO₃ + H₂O is violently exothermic → uncontrollable mist.`},{q:`The product of SO₃ + H₂SO₄ is called:`,opts:[`Dilute acid`,`Oleum (H₂S₂O₇)`,`Sodium sulphate`,`Sulphur trioxide`],ans:1,hint:`Oleum (fuming sulphuric acid) is then diluted with water.`},{q:`The main use of ammonia is:`,opts:[`Cleaning`,`Fertiliser production`,`Explosives`,`Fuel`],ans:1,hint:`NH₃ → ammonium nitrate, ammonium sulphate, urea.`},{q:`Ammonium sulphate is a good fertiliser because:`,opts:[`It is insoluble`,`It provides N and S in forms plants can absorb`,`It is acidic`,`It contains potassium`],ans:1,hint:`NH₄⁺ provides nitrogen; SO₄²⁻ provides sulphur.`},{q:`NOₓ contributes to:`,opts:[`Ozone layer repair`,`Acid rain and photochemical smog`,`Global cooling`,`Fertiliser production`],ans:1,hint:`NO₂ → HNO₃ (acid rain) and brown smog.`},{q:`Eutrophication is caused by:`,opts:[`SO₂ emissions`,`Excess nitrates in waterways`,`CO₂ in the atmosphere`,`Lead in petrol`],ans:1,hint:`Nitrate runoff → algal bloom → O₂ depletion → fish death.`},{q:`The correct order of eutrophication is:`,opts:[`Nitrate → algae die → O₂ drop`,`Nitrate → algal bloom → algae die → bacteria use O₂ → aquatic life dies`,`Algae → nitrate → rain`,`O₂ → nitrate → algae`],ans:1,hint:`The full chain must be stated for exam marks.`},{q:`SO₂ dissolves in water to form:`,opts:[`H₂SO₄`,`H₂SO₃`,`H₂S`,`Na₂SO₃`],ans:1,hint:`SO₂ + H₂O → H₂SO₃ (sulphurous acid).`},{q:`In a catalytic converter, 2CO + 2NO →:`,opts:[`2CO₂ + N₂`,`CO₂ + NO₂`,`2CO₂ + 2NO₂`,`C + O₂ + N₂`],ans:0,hint:`CO is oxidised to CO₂; NO is reduced to N₂.`},{q:`Leaded petrol damages catalytic converters because:`,opts:[`Lead is too heavy`,`Lead poisons the Pt/Rh catalyst by blocking active sites`,`Lead reacts with petrol`,`Lead is radioactive`],ans:1,hint:`Pb adsorbs on catalyst surface → blocks reactions.`},{q:`Flue gas desulphurisation uses:`,opts:[`Platinum`,`CaO or CaCO₃ to absorb SO₂`,`Iron catalyst`,`Water only`],ans:1,hint:`CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃ removes SO₂ from exhaust.`},{q:`Alkaline scrubbing removes SO₂ by:`,opts:[`Burning it`,`Reacting with NaOH: SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O`,`Cooling it`,`Adding Cl₂`],ans:1,hint:`Base neutralises the acidic SO₂.`},{q:`SO₂ preserves food by acting as:`,opts:[`An acid`,`An antioxidant and antimicrobial`,`A sweetener`,`A colourant`],ans:1,hint:`Prevents oxidation (browning) and inhibits microbial growth.`},{q:`The equation for preparing ammonium sulphate is:`,opts:[`NH₃ + HCl → NH₄Cl`,`2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄`,`NH₃ + NaOH → NaNH₂ + H₂O`,`N₂ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄`],ans:1,hint:`Ammonia + sulphuric acid → ammonium sulphate.`},{q:`Acid rain corrodes:`,opts:[`Plastic`,`Limestone buildings and statues`,`Glass`,`Stainless steel`],ans:1,hint:`CaCO₃ + H₂SO₄ → CaSO₄ + CO₂ + H₂O.`},{q:`The Haber process equation is:`,opts:[`N₂ + O₂ → 2NO`,`N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃`,`2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃`,`4NH₃ + 5O₂ → 4NO + 6H₂O`],ans:1,hint:`The reversible synthesis of ammonia from its elements.`},{q:`The forward reaction in the Haber process is:`,opts:[`Endothermic`,`Exothermic (ΔH = −92 kJ)`,`Thermoneutral`,`Variable`],ans:1,hint:`Negative ΔH = exothermic.`},{q:`The key step in the Contact process is:`,opts:[`S + O₂ → SO₂`,`2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃`,`SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄`,`H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄`],ans:1,hint:`The reversible catalytic oxidation — the equilibrium step.`},{q:`A lean-burn engine reduces NOₓ by:`,opts:[`Using more fuel`,`Using excess air to lower combustion temperature`,`Adding catalysts to the fuel`,`Removing the exhaust`],ans:1,hint:`Lower temperature → less N₂ + O₂ → 2NO.`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`Haber process conditions`,marks:9,q:`The Haber process: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain why a temperature of 450 °C is used, with reference to both kinetics and equilibria.`,marks:3,ans:`Equilibria: the forward reaction is exothermic, so Le Chatelier predicts that LOW temperature favours NH₃ (shifts right). However, Kinetics: at low temperature, the rate is too slow because few molecules have energy ≥ Ea to break the very strong N≡N bond (944 kJ mol⁻¹). 450 °C is a COMPROMISE — high enough for a reasonable rate, but not so high that yield drops too much.`},{q:`(b) Explain why a pressure of 200 atm is used.`,marks:2,ans:`4 moles of gas on the left, 2 on the right. By Le Chatelier, increasing pressure shifts equilibrium towards fewer moles → RIGHT → more NH₃. 200 atm gives a good yield. Higher pressure would increase yield further, but the cost of building and maintaining high-pressure equipment, and safety risks, make it uneconomical.`},{q:`(c) State the role of the iron catalyst.`,marks:2,ans:`The iron catalyst increases the RATE at which equilibrium is reached by lowering the activation energy. It provides a surface for N₂ and H₂ to adsorb and react (heterogeneous catalysis). It does NOT change the position of equilibrium or the value of Kc/Kp — the yield at equilibrium is the same with or without catalyst.`},{q:`(d) Explain why unreacted N₂ and H₂ are recycled.`,marks:2,ans:`The yield per pass is only about 15% — most of the N₂ and H₂ pass through unreacted. By condensing and removing the NH₃ (which liquefies at a higher temperature), then recycling the unreacted gases back through the reactor, the overall conversion approaches 98%. This makes the process economically viable.`}]},{title:`Contact process`,marks:8,q:`Sulphuric acid is manufactured by the Contact process.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write equations for each of the three stages.`,marks:3,ans:`Stage 1: S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g). Stage 2: 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g) [V₂O₅ catalyst, 450 °C]. Stage 3: SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇ (oleum); then H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄.`},{q:`(b) Explain why low pressure (1–2 atm) is used in Stage 2, despite Le Chatelier predicting that high pressure would increase yield.`,marks:2,ans:`The equilibrium constant Kp for Stage 2 is already very large, meaning the equilibrium strongly favours SO₃ at 450 °C. The conversion is 96–98% even at low pressure. Using high pressure would give only a marginal increase in yield but at enormous cost for high-pressure equipment. Low pressure is therefore economically preferable.`},{q:`(c) Explain why SO₃ is dissolved in concentrated H₂SO₄ and not directly in water.`,marks:2,ans:`Dissolving SO₃ directly in water (SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄) is extremely exothermic and produces a fine acid MIST that is very difficult to condense or contain. By dissolving SO₃ in concentrated H₂SO₄ first to form oleum (H₂S₂O₇), the reaction is controlled and safe. The oleum is then carefully diluted with water.`},{q:`(d) Name the catalyst used in Stage 2.`,marks:1,ans:`Vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅.`}]},{title:`Ammonia and ammonium ions`,marks:8,q:`Ammonia is a weak base.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Draw a dot-and-cross diagram for NH₃ and explain why it acts as a base.`,marks:2,ans:`NH₃ has 3 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair around N (pyramidal shape). The lone pair can be donated to an H⁺ ion (proton acceptor = Brønsted-Lowry base): NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺.`},{q:`(b) Describe the bonding in NH₄⁺ and state its shape.`,marks:2,ans:`NH₄⁺ has 4 covalent bonds. Three are ordinary covalent (shared pairs). The fourth is a DATIVE (coordinate) bond — both electrons came from the N lone pair. Once formed, all four N–H bonds are identical. Shape: tetrahedral, bond angle 109.5°.`},{q:`(c) Describe a test for ammonium ions in a solution.`,marks:2,ans:`Add NaOH(aq) to the solution and WARM gently. If NH₄⁺ is present, ammonia gas is released: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Test the gas with damp red litmus paper — it turns BLUE (NH₃ is alkaline).`},{q:`(d) Write the equation for the laboratory preparation of ammonia from ammonium chloride.`,marks:2,ans:`2NH₄Cl(s) + Ca(OH)₂(s) → CaCl₂(s) + 2H₂O(l) + 2NH₃(g). Heat gently. Collect by upward delivery. Dry over CaO (not CaCl₂).`}]},{title:`Environmental impacts`,marks:9,q:`Nitrogen and sulphur compounds cause environmental problems.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Explain how nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) form in car engines.`,marks:2,ans:`At the very high temperatures inside the engine (>2000 °C), nitrogen and oxygen from the air react: N₂ + O₂ → 2NO. This does not normally happen at room temperature because the N≡N bond is too strong, but the extreme heat provides enough energy to overcome the activation energy.`},{q:`(b) Describe how NOₓ leads to acid rain.`,marks:2,ans:`NO is oxidised in the atmosphere: 2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂. NO₂ then dissolves in rainwater and reacts with O₂: 4NO₂ + O₂ + 2H₂O → 4HNO₃. The nitric acid makes the rain acidic (pH < 5.6).`},{q:`(c) Explain the process of eutrophication caused by nitrate fertiliser runoff.`,marks:3,ans:`Excess nitrate fertiliser washes off farmland into rivers and lakes. The high nitrate concentration causes rapid growth of algae (algal bloom). When the algae die, bacteria decompose them. The bacteria use large amounts of dissolved oxygen for aerobic respiration. This depletes the O₂ in the water, causing fish and other aquatic organisms to suffocate and die. The water becomes stagnant and lifeless.`},{q:`(d) Explain how catalytic converters reduce NOₓ emissions.`,marks:2,ans:`The exhaust gases pass over Pt/Pd/Rh catalysts on a honeycomb support (large surface area). The key reaction is: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂. NO is reduced to harmless N₂; CO is simultaneously oxidised to CO₂. This is heterogeneous catalysis — the toxic gases adsorb on the catalyst surface, react, and the products desorb.`}]},{title:`SO₂ emission control`,marks:7,q:`Power stations burning coal produce SO₂.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the formation of SO₂ from burning sulphur impurities in coal.`,marks:1,ans:`S(s) + O₂(g) → SO₂(g).`},{q:`(b) Explain how flue gas desulphurisation removes SO₂. Give an equation.`,marks:2,ans:`The exhaust gases are passed through powdered CaO or a CaCO₃ slurry. SO₂ reacts with the basic oxide/carbonate: CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃. The CaSO₃ can be oxidised to CaSO₄ (gypsum), which has commercial uses (plasterboard).`},{q:`(c) Describe alkaline scrubbing as an alternative method.`,marks:2,ans:`The exhaust gas is passed through a solution of NaOH: SO₂ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₃ + H₂O. The alkaline solution neutralises the acidic SO₂. This method is effective but more expensive than limestone-based methods.`},{q:`(d) Explain one use of SO₂ in the food industry.`,marks:2,ans:`SO₂ is used as a food preservative (E220). It acts as an antioxidant, preventing browning of dried fruits and fruit juices by reacting with oxygen before the food can oxidise. It also has antimicrobial properties, inhibiting the growth of bacteria and moulds, extending shelf life.`}]},{title:`Comparing Haber and Contact`,marks:8,q:`Both the Haber and Contact processes are reversible exothermic reactions.`,parts:[{q:`(a) State the equation, catalyst and operating conditions for each process.`,marks:4,ans:`Haber: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. Catalyst: Fe. T: 450 °C. P: 200 atm. Contact (Stage 2): 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃. Catalyst: V₂O₅. T: 450 °C. P: 1–2 atm.`},{q:`(b) Explain why the Haber process uses high pressure but the Contact process uses low pressure.`,marks:2,ans:`Haber: 4 mol gas → 2 mol gas. High pressure shifts equilibrium right, significantly increasing yield. The improvement justifies the cost. Contact: 3 mol gas → 2 mol gas. The equilibrium already strongly favours SO₃ (Kp is very large at 450 °C). High pressure would give only a marginal improvement, not worth the cost.`},{q:`(c) Explain why both processes use the same temperature (450 °C) despite both being exothermic.`,marks:2,ans:`Both are exothermic, so Le Chatelier predicts that lower temperatures would give higher yields. However, at lower temperatures, the rate is too slow for industrial production. 450 °C is a compromise in both cases — high enough for the catalyst to work effectively and provide a workable rate, while still giving a reasonable equilibrium yield.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Explain Haber conditions`,diff:`medium`,q:`Explain why a temperature of 450 °C is used in the Haber process.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`The forward reaction (N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃) is exothermic (ΔH = −92 kJ mol⁻¹).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`By Le Chatelier, low temperature would shift equilibrium RIGHT → higher yield of NH₃.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`BUT at low temperature, the rate is very slow because the N≡N bond (944 kJ mol⁻¹) requires high Ea to break.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`450 °C is a COMPROMISE: fast enough for industry, reasonable yield (~15% per pass, ~98% overall with recycling).`}],ans:`450 °C is a compromise between yield (low T better) and rate (high T better).`},{title:`Contact process Stage 3`,diff:`medium`,q:`Why is SO₃ dissolved in conc. H₂SO₄ rather than water?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`The reaction SO₃ + H₂O → H₂SO₄ is extremely exothermic.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`If SO₃ is added directly to water, the heat generated is so intense that it boils the water, producing a fine acid MIST.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`This mist is dangerous and very difficult to condense or contain.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Instead, SO₃ is dissolved in conc. H₂SO₄ → oleum (H₂S₂O₇). This is a controlled reaction. Oleum is then carefully diluted with water: H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄.`}],ans:`SO₃ + H₂O directly → dangerous acid mist. Dissolving in H₂SO₄ first → controllable.`},{title:`Test for NH₄⁺`,diff:`easy`,q:`Describe how to test for ammonium ions in a solution.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Add NaOH(aq) to the test solution.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Warm gently.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`If NH₄⁺ is present: NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Ammonia gas is released.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Hold damp RED litmus paper over the tube mouth. If it turns BLUE, NH₃ is confirmed.`}],ans:`Add NaOH, warm. Damp red litmus turns blue → NH₄⁺ confirmed.`},{title:`Eutrophication chain`,diff:`medium`,q:`Explain the process of eutrophication caused by nitrate fertilisers.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Excess nitrate fertiliser runs off farmland into rivers/lakes.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`High nitrate levels → rapid growth of algae (algal bloom) on the water surface.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Algae block light from reaching submerged plants, which die. The algae eventually die too.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Decomposition bacteria break down the dead algae/plants. They consume large amounts of dissolved O₂ for aerobic respiration.`},{label:`STEP 5`,text:`O₂ levels drop drastically → fish and other aquatic organisms suffocate and die. The body of water becomes "dead".`}],ans:`Nitrate → algal bloom → algae die → bacteria use O₂ → fish die.`},{title:`FGD equation`,diff:`easy`,q:`Write the equation for flue gas desulphurisation using CaO.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`SO₂ is an acidic oxide. CaO is a basic oxide.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`They react in a neutralisation: CaO(s) + SO₂(g) → CaSO₃(s).`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`The CaSO₃ can be further oxidised to CaSO₄ (gypsum) for commercial use.`}],ans:`CaO + SO₂ → CaSO₃. Acid-base neutralisation removes SO₂ from flue gas.`},{title:`Catalytic converter reactions`,diff:`medium`,q:`Write the key equation for a catalytic converter and explain the catalyst type.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Main reaction: 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`CO is oxidised (+2 → +4). NO is reduced (+2 → 0). Both toxic gases converted to harmless products.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Catalyst: Pt/Pd/Rh on a honeycomb support (large surface area).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Heterogeneous catalysis: gaseous reactants adsorb on the solid catalyst surface.`}],ans:`2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂. Pt/Pd/Rh, heterogeneous catalysis.`},{title:`Compare Haber and Contact pressures`,diff:`hard`,q:`Explain why the Haber process uses 200 atm but the Contact process uses only 1–2 atm.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Haber: N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃. Δn = 2 − 4 = −2. Pressure has a BIG effect (4 → 2 moles gas). High pressure significantly increases yield.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`At 200 atm, the equilibrium shift provides a worthwhile increase in NH₃ yield, justifying the cost.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Contact: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃. Δn = 2 − 3 = −1. Pressure effect is less dramatic. More importantly, Kp is ALREADY VERY LARGE at 450 °C.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`This means the equilibrium already strongly favours SO₃ (~98% conversion). High pressure would give only ~99% — not worth the enormous cost. So 1–2 atm is used.`}],ans:`Haber: large Δn and moderate K → high P worthwhile. Contact: large Kp already → high P unnecessary.`},{title:`Ammonium sulphate preparation`,diff:`easy`,q:`Write the equation for making ammonium sulphate and outline the lab method.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`2NH₃(aq) + H₂SO₄(aq) → (NH₄)₂SO₄(aq).`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Titrate NH₃ solution with H₂SO₄ using methyl orange indicator to find the endpoint volume.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Repeat using the exact volumes WITHOUT indicator to get a pure solution.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Evaporate gently to crystallisation point, cool, filter, wash, dry.`}],ans:`2NH₃ + H₂SO₄ → (NH₄)₂SO₄. Titration method → crystallisation.`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through the Haber process conditions using kinetics AND equilibria`,`Why does the Contact process use low pressure but the Haber uses high?`,`Explain eutrophication step by step`,`How does a catalytic converter work? Give equations.`,`What happens when SO₃ is dissolved in water vs in conc. H₂SO₄?`]};export{e as NSULPHUR};